			<!-- Section 5: Professional Cutting Guide -->
			<section id="cutting-guide" style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					5. How to Cut a Tiered Wedding Cake (Concentric Circles Method)
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					Never cut a large wedding cake like a standard birthday pie into triangular wedges! Cutting pie wedges on cakes larger than 8 inches results in massive, floppy 6-inch-long slices that break apart. Caterers and venues use the <strong>Concentric Ring Method</strong>:
				</p>
				
				<ol class="font-body" style="padding-left: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 1.05rem;">
					<li><strong>Remove Top Tier & Dowels:</strong> Gently lift off the 6-inch top tier to be boxed for the couple. Remove internal wooden dowels before slicing each tier.</li>
					<li><strong>Cut an Inner Circle:</strong> For any tier 10 inches or larger, insert a sharp chef knife 2 inches in from the outer edge and cut a complete circle all the way around.</li>
					<li><strong>Slice the Outer Ring:</strong> Cut the outer 2-inch wide ring into uniform 1-inch slices (yielding 1" × 2" slices).</li>
					<li><strong>Repeat for Larger Tiers:</strong> For massive 14" or 16" tiers, cut a second concentric ring 2 inches deeper.</li>
					<li><strong>Slice the Center:</strong> Once only the 6-inch core cylinder remains, cut it straight across into halves and slice horizontally into uniform portions.</li>
				</ol>
			</section>

