				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; text-align: center;">
					Frequently Asked Questions
				</h3>

				<details class="faq-accordion" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--bg-paper);">
					<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent-blue);">
						How big of a wedding cake do I need for 100 to 110 guests?
					</summary>
					<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue); font-size: 1.05rem;">
						For 100 guests, the most popular choice is a <strong>3-tier round cake measuring 6", 9", and 12"</strong>, which provides exactly 100 wedding slices (1"x2"). If you have 110 guests, an 8", 10", and 12" 3-tier cake provides 118 servings, giving you a comfortable safety cushion without overpaying.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="faq-accordion" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--bg-paper);">
					<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent-blue);">
						Should we count the top tier in our servings?
					</summary>
					<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue); font-size: 1.05rem;">
						If you are following the tradition of freezing the 6-inch top tier to eat on your 1st anniversary or at your first child's christening, <strong>do not count it</strong> toward guest servings! Subtract 12 servings from your total cake yield, or check the "Save Top Tier" box in our calculator above.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="faq-accordion" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--bg-paper);">
					<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent-blue);">
						How many slices do square and heart-shaped cakes yield?
					</summary>
					<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue); font-size: 1.05rem;">
						Square cakes yield approximately <strong>25% more servings</strong> than round cakes of the same diameter because corners provide extra volume. For instance, a 10" round cake feeds 38 people, whereas a 10" square cake yields 50 servings. Heart-shaped cakes yield roughly 10% fewer servings than round cakes due to the carved top indentation.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="faq-accordion" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--bg-paper);">
					<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent-blue);">
						What is the best way to keep wedding cake fresh?
					</summary>
					<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue); font-size: 1.05rem;">
						Keep the tiered cake in a cool, air-conditioned room out of direct sunlight. Buttercream melts above 75°F (24°C). If freezing the top tier for your anniversary, chill it uncovered for 2 hours until the frosting hardens, wrap tightly in 3 layers of plastic wrap followed by 2 layers of heavy aluminum foil, and place inside an airtight freezer container.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="faq-accordion" style="margin-bottom: 1rem; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--bg-paper);">
					<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent-blue);">
						Do caterers charge a cake cutting fee?
					</summary>
					<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue); font-size: 1.05rem;">
						Yes, many reception venues and caterers charge a <strong>cake cutting fee between $1.50 and $3.50 per guest</strong> if you bring a cake from an outside bakery. This fee covers slicing, plating, providing dessert forks, and serving each guest. Check your catering contract ahead of time!
					</p>
				</details>

			</section>

		</article>

	</main>
</Layout>

